[18:39:11] [METRIC] [n1]   12,500,000/50,000,000 rows (596 MiB) @ 23.5 MB/s  ETA 67s
...
[18:42:08] [OK    ] [n1] BULK COPY committed: 2.33 GiB in 285.1s (8.4 MB/s, 78 flushes)
[18:42:08] [HEADER] WAITING FOR CONVERGENCE on n2 (expected 50,000,000 rows...)
[18:42:34] [METRIC] [n2] 24,891,234/50,000,000 (49.8%) ETA 12s @ 1,920,000 rows/s on n2
...
[18:42:46] [HEADER] VERDICT
[18:42:46] [INFO  ]   n1 throughput        : 175,432 rows/s (8.4 MB/s)
[18:42:46] [INFO  ]   n2 apply throughput  : 1,920,489 rows/s (1095% of n1)
[18:42:46] [OK    ] RESULT: PASS — replicated, converged, md5 matches
```

## Caveats

- **Schema is fixed**: `(id bigint PK, payload bytea, checksum text)`. The
  table is dropped and recreated at the start of every run.
- **Server-side MD5** of the full table is run at convergence — slow for
  multi-GiB tables but tractable up to ~20 GiB.
- **Spock subscription must already exist** between n2 and n1. The script
  doesn't create subscriptions; it only adds the test table to the repset.
- **The user in the config needs superuser** for tablespace/role operations
  during chaos. For pure data tests, repset member privileges are enough.
- **Spock 6.x compatibility**: the script auto-detects column-name differences
  between Spock 5.x (`sub_name`/`sub_enabled`) and 6.x (`slot_name`/`status`).
- **Memory**: the producer flushes the COPY buffer every 32 MiB regardless of
  row size, so memory stays bounded even for large per-row payloads.

## Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | Run passed (success or expected failure) |
| 1 | Run failed (n1 errored unexpectedly, or n2 didn't converge) |
| 2 | Config / flag error before the test started |
| 130 | Interrupted by Ctrl-C |